 train in the two days?\nAnswer:", 'gold': '230', 'pred': '160', 'completion': ' The first train covers 80 miles in the first day.\nThe second train covers 150 miles in the first day.\nThe total distance covered by the first train is 80 + 80 = <<80+80=160>>160 miles.\nThe total distance covered by the second train is 150 + 150 = <<150+150=300>>300 miles.\nThe distance covered by the first train is 160 - 300 = <<160-300=-140>>140 miles.\nThe distance covered by the second train is 300 - 140 = <<300-140=160>>160 miles.\n#### 160'}, {'prompt': "Question: Jill gets paid $20 per hour to teach and $30 to be a cheerleading coach. If she works 50 weeks a year, 35 hours a week as a teacher and 15 hours a week as a coach, what's her annual salary?\nAnswer:", 'gold': '57500', 'pred': '2050', 'completion': ' Jill gets paid $20 per hour to teach, so she gets paid $20 x 50 weeks = $<<20*50=1000>>1000 per year.\nShe gets paid $30 per hour to be a coach, so she gets paid $30 x 35 weeks = $<<30*35=1050>>1050 per year.\nHer total annual salary is $1000 + $1050 = $<<1000+1050=2050>>2050.\n#### 2050'}, {'prompt': 'Question: Claire makes a 3 egg omelet every morning for breakfast.  How many dozens of eggs will she eat in 4 weeks?\nAnswer:', 'gold': '7', 'pred': '12', 'completion': ' In a week, Claire eats 3 eggs of eggs every day, so in 4 weeks she eats 3*4=<<3*4=12>>12 eggs of eggs.\n#### 12'}, {'prompt': 'Question: Marissa is hiking a 12-mile trail. She took 1 hour to walk the first 4 miles, then another hour to walk the next two miles. If she wants her average speed to be 4 miles per hour, what speed (in miles per hour) does she need to walk the remaining distance?\nAnswer:', 'gold': '6', 'pred': '6', 'completion': ' She walked 4 miles in the first hour and 2 miles in the second hour, so she walked 4+2= <<4+2=6>>6 miles in 2 hours.\nShe needs to walk 12-6=<<12-6=6>>6 miles in 1 hour.\nHer average speed is 6 miles per hour.\n#### 6'}]}
